        "heading": "",
        "intro": "",
        "html": "<p>We don&#39;t sell gloves yet, on purpose. We won&#39;t put our name on a pair until they&#39;re as good as the gloves serious keepers already wear. That also means we&#39;ve got no reason to push you towards any brand. Here&#39;s how to choose.</p>\n<h2>1. Surface first</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Natural grass (dry or wet):</strong> any latex works. Softer latex grips best.</li>\n<li><strong>Artificial grass (3G/4G):</strong> rubber crumb wears latex fast. Use tougher training latex, or accept that soft match gloves won&#39;t last long.</li>\n<li><strong>Indoor/hard courts:</strong> abrasion is the problem. Use tougher latex, and be wary of anything with a very soft palm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you play on more than one surface, the best fix isn&#39;t a magic glove. It&#39;s <strong>two pairs</strong>.</p>\n<h2>2. Cut: how the glove fits your hand</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Cut</th>\n<th>Feels like</th>\n<th>Good for</th>\n<th>Trade-off</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Negative</strong></td>\n<td>Tight, seams on the inside</td>\n<td>Feel and control. Popular with keepers who like a close fit.</td>\n<td>Less latex contact area. Can feel tight at first.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Roll finger</strong></td>\n<td>Latex wraps around the fingers</td>\n<td>Maximum latex contact when catching</td>\n<td>Slightly less snug feel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Flat palm</strong></td>\n<td>Traditional, a little roomier</td>\n<td>Comfort, wider hands</td>\n<td>Less close control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hybrid</strong></td>\n<td>Mixes of the above</td>\n<td>Getting the best of two cuts</td>\n<td>Depends heavily on the brand</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>There&#39;s no &quot;best&quot; cut. Try what feels natural when your hand is in a catching shape.</p>\n<h2>3. Latex: soft grips, hard lasts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Soft/&quot;contact&quot; latex:</strong> maximum grip, wears fastest. Best for matches on grass.</li>\n<li><strong>Harder/training latex:</strong> less grip, lasts much longer. Best for training and turf.</li>\n<li><strong>Thickness:</strong> thicker latex cushions more and usually lasts longer, but reduces feel.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>4. Fingersaves (spines)</h2>\n<p>Stiffeners that stop fingers bending backwards. They&#39;re useful if you&#39;ve had finger injuries or are recovering from one. Many keepers find they reduce feel and flexibility, so don&#39;t buy them &quot;just in case&quot; unless that trade-off suits you.</p>\n<h2>5. Wrist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Strap (hook-and-loop):</strong> adjustable, secure, the most common.</li>\n<li><strong>Bandage/wrap style:</strong> very secure wrist support.</li>\n<li><strong>Strapless/sock-style:</strong> light, and easy on and off. Less adjustable.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>6. Size — measure, don&#39;t guess</h2>\n<p>Measure around your hand below the knuckles (thumb out). Your glove size is roughly that measurement in inches. Brands vary by up to half a size, so check the brand chart too. <a href=\"/pages/size-guide\">Use our size calculator →</a></p>\n<h2>What&#39;s worth paying for (and what isn&#39;t)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Worth it:</strong> good latex on your match gloves, a proper fit, and a second pair for training.</li>\n<li><strong>Often not worth it:</strong> top-of-range gloves for training on turf, fingersaves you don&#39;t need, or buying a size up &quot;to grow into&quot; (a loose glove costs you catches).</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<p><em>One day we&#39;ll make our own gloves, when we find a manufacturer who can actually do them right. Not before. Until then: <a href=\"/collections/glove-care\">make the pair you own last longer →</a></em></p>",
        "narrow": true,
        "tone": "ink",
